= The Fall of Marx IV

Following last year's controversial civil service reforms—strongly opposed by Stegerwald and the trade union leadership—the reignition of class conflict has proven to be detrimental to internal party unity. In response, the national leadership has scrambled to find a unifying policy agreeable to the divergent wings of the party: a comprehensive Reich School Law. Such a measure would have united the party through shared religious concerns and Kulterpolitik, while also having a greater chance of passing given the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span>’s presence in government.

Under Article 174 of the <span style="color: #000000;">We</span><span style="color: #DD0000;">im</span><span style="color: #FFCC00;">ar</span> Constitution, Germany's education system has remained under control of the Länder, preserving the pre-war arrangement. While this served as a needed compromise among the <span style="color: #000000;">We</span><span style="color: #DD0000;">im</span><span style="color: #FFCC00;">ar</span> coalition partners, it has also led to a noticeable lack of denominational schools in Catholic-majority states such as Baden and Hesse. 

With the aid of Walter von Keudell of the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span>, we drafted a school law enshrining the primacy of interdenominational schools throughout Germany. However, the anti-clerical <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span> has voiced strong opposition, even threatening to bring down the coalition if the legislation proceeds.

- @reject: The <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span> expresses their outrage
